= Computer network — Technical =

== Packet switching ==
Many modern networks divide data into packets routed or switched independently.

Packet switching multiplexes links statistically but introduces queueing, loss, reordering and variable latency.

== Layered model ==
An Internet stack can be summarized:

<pre>
application
transport
network
link
physical medium
</pre>

Encapsulation lets each layer carry the data of higher layers.

== Routing and forwarding ==
Forwarding moves packets using local tables.

Routing computes or distributes information used to construct those paths.

The two functions are related but distinct.

== Transport ==
Transport protocols provide end-to-end services such as reliability, ordering, multiplexing and congestion control.

Different applications require different tradeoffs.

== Performance ==
Key quantities include latency, bandwidth, throughput, loss, jitter and goodput.

High bandwidth does not imply low latency.

